1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 5. Nb5 a6 (5... d6 6. N1c3 a6 7. Na3
b5 8. Nd5 Nge7 9. c4 Nxd5 10. exd5 Nd4 11. cxb5 Be7 12. Bd3 {Here the kings do not yet feel safe. Both sides have checks available.} O-O 13. O-O
{Example of a middlegame: the king is safe and the pieces dominate the board.}) 6.
Nd6+ Bxd6 7. Qxd6 Qf6 8. Qxf6 Nxf6 {After the queens are exchanged, the kings feel very safe. They no longer need to be brought to safety, so the opening is over. Since the kings can even move around safely, we have almost reached the endgame.} 9. Nc3 Nb4 {In the middlegame/endgame, a piece may be moved twice.} 10. Bd3 Nxd3+ 11. cxd3 d6 12. Bg5 (12. O-O $6 {The king is worse placed on g1. On e1 it is more active, which is more important.}) (12. Rf1 {If White wants to play f4, the king does not need to be on g1.}) 12... Be6 13. Rc1 Rc8 14.
Kd2 $1 {The king remains in the center because this is an endgame.} O-O $2 {Black makes a mistake. The king is sidelined on g8. Sample line:} 15. a3
h6 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Na4 Rxc1 18. Rxc1 Rc8 19. Rxc8+ Bxc8 20. Kc3 {White has an advantage because the king joins the battle.} *

