[Black "Golden Opening Rules"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[ECO "B00"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{The aim of the opening is to bring the pieces into the battle quickly. The golden rules provide a useful guide. They are covered in detail in Part 7. According to them, an opening move is good if it follows one of three principles: 1. Center 2. Development 3. King safety. There is also a hierarchy: center > development > king safety. *The golden rules are a good guideline for beginners and reflect the classical understanding of chess. Modern openings are not covered by them. **Regardless of this, active moves should be checked first and again after choosing a move! The golden rules only help with the plan.} 1. e4 *

[Event "-OPENING-"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Golden Opening Rules"]
[Black "1. Center"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[ECO "B00"]
[PlyCount "4"]

1. e4 e6 (1... g6 $2 {According to the golden rules—and in my view as well—this is both a mistake and, more importantly, not advisable for non-professionals. Black already violates a very important fundamental principle: the center.} 2. d4 Bg7 {White has complete control and freedom in the center.}) (1...
b6 $2 2. d4 Bb7 {White has the center and complete control.} 3. Nd2 $5 {[%cal Gc2c3,Yf1d3] White does not always have to place the pieces on their 'ideal squares'.} (3.
Nc3) (3. Bd3)) 2. d4 d6 $2 {White has the center. (How to exploit the central advantage concretely is explained from Part 10 onward.)} (2... d5 $1 {Black absolutely must fight for at least one of the two central squares.}) *

[Event "-OPENING-"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Golden Opening Rules"]
[Black "2. Development"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "15"]

{The second rule is development. This means developing the pieces quickly to useful squares. You should also avoid moving the same piece twice, because you could develop another piece instead.} 
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 (2. Ne2 $2 {According to the rule, this is a mistake because the knight is poorly placed on e2 and obstructs the other pieces.}) (2. d4 $6 exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 {Black has developed with tempo, which is not good for White.}) 2... d6 $6 {According to the rule, this is a small mistake because the bishop on f8 can no longer reach c5. It is less well developed on e7.} (2... Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Nxd4 $2 5.
Qxd4 {Black cannot drive away the white queen. It is well developed and very strong there.}) 3. Bc4 Nd7 $6 {The knight blocks the bishop on c8 here. Why not Nc6?} 4. d4 {First rule: the center!} exd4 5. Nxd4 (5. Qxd4 $5 {The queen is not in danger here.}) 5... Nc5 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. O-O {Black has lost so much time that Black cannot afford to open the center.} Nfxe4 $2 (
7... Be7 {Black has no choice but to develop and secure the king.})
8. Re1 $18 *

