[PlyCount "10"]

{My first guideline for you—a guideline that can later be expanded all the way to GM level (from Part 10 onward), but is initially greatly simplified—is: 1. Check active moves. If you find a satisfactory continuation, play it. 2. (If step 1 yields nothing:) Continue with your plan. If you currently have no plan, use the activation of your pieces as an emergency plan. 3. Before playing your move, always check the opponent's active replies to it. If it is dangerous, return to step 2 and find an alternative.} 10. O-O O-O {The opening is complete. If White has a plan, simply carry it out; otherwise, simply activate the pieces.} 11. Be3 (11. Bg5 {is also good}) 11... Re8
12. Nbd2 Be6 {Again: 1. Active moves: none. 2. Plan: for example, besiege d5; or 2b: place the rooks on open files. 3. Check whether Bxh3 or something similar creates a problem.} 13. Rfe1 a6 14. Rad1 Rc8 {If there is still no plan, then Nd4 or Bd4, because that improves the pieces. And so on.} *

