[PlyCount "0"]

{Position from Capablanca–Ragozin. The endgame is covered in detail in Part 20. --- In the endgame, play proceeds much more slowly than in the middlegame. There is no bloodbath; attention is paid to even the smallest weaknesses. A pawn can decide the game through promotion. Pawns are the heroes of the endgame. They become stronger because there is time to invest in their slow advance. The king must obviously participate actively as well. The most important objective in the endgame is either to win pawns or to promote them. The rest is explored in Part 20.}  *

[Event "-ENDGAME-"]
[Site "?"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Endgame"]
[Black "Small Weaknesses Decide"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "ChessTutor"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/5pkp/2p3p1/pp6/4Pn2/1P3P2/P1B2P1P/7K w - - 0 29"]
[PlyCount "0"]

{In this position, White merely has a bad bishop and doubled pawns on the f-file. In the middlegame, White could develop counterplay or drive away the knight. Here in the endgame, however, these are not secondary but the most important features. Black wins easily with ...Kf6-e5-d4 while White can do absolutely nothing. Black heads to b2 and attacks the white pawns, or creates a passed pawn on the c-file.}  *

