{Game Krassowizkij, J (2350)–Endisch, P (1950), 2011} 11... f5 $2 12. Bxd5 $1
Bxd5 13. Qxd5 Qxd5 14. Nc7+ Kf7 15. Nxd5 {White has won a pawn. The only aim is to convert it in the endgame.} Rc8 16. Rd1 {Active pieces are always necessary.} Nf6 17. Nc3 {I do not want to explain this move here; it is harder to understand. The aim is to give Black less counterplay.} Rhd8 18. Bg5 (18. Rxd8 Rxd8 {White gives Black the d-file here.}) 18... Ne4 $5 19. Nxe4 fxe4 20. Nd2 Bxb2 21. Rab1 Bd4 22. Nxe4 {Now the aim is to exchange pieces.} b6 23. cxb6 Bxb6 24. Rxd8 {first rook} Nxd8 25.
Rc1 {second rook} Ra8 {Because White placed the rook centrally, Black must either exchange it or move the pieces away.} (25... Rb8 26. Bf4)
26. Nc5 Nc6 27. Nd7 Nd4 28. Nxb6 axb6 {Black has some counterplay, but White's pieces are more active.} 29. Rc7 Rxa2 30. Rxe7+ Kg8 31. Kf1 {White has a bishop against a knight and two passed pawns against one.} b5 32. Bf6 {wins another pawn} Nc6 33. Rg7+ Kf8 34. Rxh7 b4 35. Rb7 Ra6 36. Bg7+ Kg8 37. Bb2 {[%cal Gb7g7] also threatens Rg7+} Ra2 38. Rb6 {exchanges more pieces} Rxb2 39.
Rxc6 Rb1+ 40. Kg2 Kf7 41. Rb6 b3 42. Kf3 {With two extra pawns, the position is now simply won.} b2 43. e4 Kg7 44. Kg4 Re1 45. Rxb2 Rxe4+ 46. f4 Re7 47.
h4 Ra7 48. h5 *

