[PlyCount "8"]

{Game Krassowizkij, J (2250)–Hasenohr, B (2150), 2011} 14... f5 $1 15. Qe2
{In contrast to the static advantage, we now have a dynamic advantage. Black has the initiative, but it is only temporary. If Black gave White time for Rfe1 and Ne5, White would even have an advantage. Black must therefore act quickly and convert the dynamic advantage into something tangible—a static advantage such as material.} c5 $1 (15... e5 $6 16. c5 $1
bxc5 17. dxe5 $14 {Black has failed to convert the advantage and is left only with static pawn weaknesses.} (17. Bc4+ $5 {A pawn sacrifice is said to be even better.} Kh8 18. dxc5 Nxc5 19. Ng5 $40)) 16. Ng5 $5 {Instead of waiting for my downfall, I decided to complicate the position. In particular, I am prepared to give up h2 to gain time for counterplay. My opponent could not cope with the complications. That is the difficulty of dynamic positions: everything is fluid, and it is easy to overlook or misjudge something. Play develops very rapidly.}
cxd4 17. Bxd4 e5 $6 {looks completely winning at first sight, but falls into my trap.} 18. c5 $3 {[%cal Gd3c4] and now White takes over the initiative. White later won this game.} *
